         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/53b93fac-09ec-40ac-bb53-45fd14657399,1792433349852/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C0%2C3021%2C1699%29%3B' alt='Inside a 2-storey home, looking up the stairs, there is a large hole where the ceiling should be, showing blue sky. There is debris all over the stairs.' width='620' height='349' title='Lawrence Huang says he has been living in a motel since contractors walked off the job after removing his roof. He says he has no way to pay for the rebuild, as his insurance company has denied his claim.'/><p>What began as a simple knock on the front door last spring has resulted in an elderly Scarborough man living out of a suitcase, his home roofless and in ruins. While police are investigating a possible fraud, the man says he can't afford to rebuild his roof with his insurance claim denied.</p>]]></description>
